Author: Martin Sedlacek <martin.martin.sedlacek@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 14 03:13:26 2026 +0200

    Place an articulation by its ROOT LINK, not by whichever prim was read
    
    REALM's impact_drawer cabinet.usd could not load. Two independent defects, both the same mistake:
    code that reads an entity's pose through the virtual accessor (root link) and writes it through a
    path pinned to XFormPrim (the entity prim). cabinet.usd is the first asset here whose base_link is
    NOT at the entity origin -- it sits at Ry(180) + 4 cm -- so it is the first asset that can tell the
    two apart. Every BEHAVIOR dataset asset has base_link at the origin, which is why this survived.
    
    1. XFormPrim._set_xform_properties read `self.get_position_orientation()` (EntityPrim -> root link)
       and wrote `XFormPrim.set_position_orientation(self, ...)` (entity prim). The write therefore
       moved the object by base_link's local transform, and the method's own round-trip assert caught
       it:
    
         AssertionError: /World/scene_0/drawer:
           old_pos tensor([0.0243, 0.8223, -0.0317])
           new_pos tensor([2.3468e-04, 8.1015e-01, -6.3757e-02])
    
       Confirmed by reconstructing both poses from the authored matrices: base_link_local @ entity_local
       reproduces old_pos/old_orn, and base_link_local @ old reproduces new_pos/new_orn, to every
       printed digit. The asymmetry came in with the v3.9.1 port (25c73e1), which pinned the write and
       left both reads virtual. Fixed by pinning the reads to match.
    
    2. EntityPrim.set_position_orientation's stopped-sim branch moves the entity prim and asserted that
       the root link has no relative pose to it. That is an OmniGibson convention, not a property of
       USD, and asserting it makes any DCC-exported articulation unloadable. The playing branch already
       does the right thing (set_world_poses addresses the root link), so this now aims the entity prim
       at target * root_local^-1, which puts the root link exactly where the caller asked. Skipped
       entirely when root_local is identity, so every asset that satisfied the old assert is written
       bit-for-bit as before.
    
    Verified on l40s-01 (alloc 190842): REALM tests/test_vector_integrity.py --cells 8:Default at
    --num_envs 2 now reports PASS (2 rollouts, all artifacts). Both tasks failed at load before.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

